/F1 18 Tf (Memory Systems and Multi-Agent Coordination) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf
T*
/F1 14 Tf (3. Memory Hierarchy in Production Agents) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf
(Agents utilize a dual-tiered memory architecture:) Tj T*
(- Working Memory: Maintains immediate turn-by-turn conversational context and tool responses.) Tj T*
(- Semantic Episodic Memory: Vector stores \(e.g. Chroma, FAISS, Milvus\) that index past interactions.) Tj T*
(Experiments demonstrate that semantic retrieval reduces hallucination rates by up to 64% in QA.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf (4. Multi-Agent Collaboration Patterns) Tj T* /F1 12 Tf
(Complex enterprise objectives exceed the capabilities of single monolithic agents.) Tj T*
(Multi-agent patterns divide labor among specialized personas:) Tj T*
(- Hierarchical Delegation: A Supervisor Agent plans and assigns tasks to subordinate workers.) Tj T*
(- Peer-to-Peer Consensus: Agents critique each others outputs via debate loops before finalizing.) Tj T*
(- Blackboard Architecture: Specialized agents post partial solutions to a shared message board.) Tj T*
(In benchmark tests, three collaborating agents \(Researcher, Analyst, Reporter\) achieved a 42%) Tj T*
(higher fact-verification score than a single zero-shot prompt.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
